#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281595 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198434 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e74c46c7-7f57-5adc-8acb-ea91958d8725" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "259e5a02-0c0f-55f3-bd6c-0425cc1b2fca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278926} {:answer/id #uuid "5764bf31-2e34-5139-b5af-336d908fb12d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279392} {:answer/id #uuid "b8015639-eca8-56a2-ba22-50eb0b6beb73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280209} {:answer/id #uuid "cceb0541-bef3-574b-9eaf-de3916b0c09d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280395} {:answer/id #uuid "f640ccfa-97c4-5abd-9713-fdd182bc2d62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "259e5a02-0c0f-55f3-bd6c-0425cc1b2fca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278926} :answer-field/id #uuid "28d25be3-23d4-5ea5-b1b7-1033574afeb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "27445c9b-7e4a-5e28-a2d1-3fc7a72547b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-163089" :question/problem "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=2{e}^{x-y},$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2+ax-2y+b{x}^{2}+cxy+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2{e}^{x-y}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-2{e}^{x-y} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =2, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =2{e}^{x-y}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-2{e}^{x-y}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-2 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =2{e}^{x-y}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈2+2\\cdot x+(-2)\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-2)\\cdot xy+2\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2+2x-2y+1{x}^{2}+(-2)xy+{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b=2+1+(-2)=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198435 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b91ae708-a63d-51ac-9ae0-f24724392858" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f322457-25fd-5a66-86e8-083d4272a096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516172} {:answer/id #uuid "45a5c347-378b-5252-8c5b-08cec2880c91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516254} {:answer/id #uuid "4a73e654-74cd-553c-bd37-5755a05e3bca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516264} {:answer/id #uuid "6f9a04e1-a076-5063-b000-58d0bd6211f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516360} {:answer/id #uuid "a5683896-77cb-5f32-b1ff-4d08b73452cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a73e654-74cd-553c-bd37-5755a05e3bca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516264} :answer-field/id #uuid "70dfe391-1be5-5314-819b-7419c185891e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f8fd8fac-7611-58e8-a16a-eb7f95407000" :question/math-academy-id "q-163132" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y),\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈∗+[\\begin{aligned}∗ & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}∗ & 3 \\\\ ∗ & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the six missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defened by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-y) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{′}(0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y) & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n1+0+x+x+(-9)+3=2x-5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Multivariable Function at a Point Using a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f8a1f57-6edf-5d51-9b7c-2d9bbd429811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281829} {:answer/id #uuid "13106fba-e2d8-590f-9a50-2b1d63582d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281872} {:answer/id #uuid "1abdb628-18f3-5897-af88-0cc7b944c171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281934} {:answer/id #uuid "2a19cffe-821c-5512-84e1-9848e5f50a0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282054} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf50d5a-a34b-5ce3-bd0f-8c170f17a78a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f8a1f57-6edf-5d51-9b7c-2d9bbd429811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281829} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d02b8fb-22de-53d2-a4aa-1c9d236c7ce2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af702ba5-d262-51dd-8530-cde831134efb" :question/math-academy-id "q-164830" :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =0, & \\,{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}$ to approximate the value of $f(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a)={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix},$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =0, & \\,\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈0+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & ≈0+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ -(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =-{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
